{{also|Equus}}
==Latin==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|la|equos}} ''(until the middle of the first century B.C.)''
* {{l|la|ecus}} ''(the regular development, later remodeled to ''equus'' on the analogy of the stem ''equ-'' as remained, e.g., in the genitive'' equi)

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|la|h₁eḱ}}
From {{der|la|itc-pro|*ekwos}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*h₁éḱwos||horse}}. Cognates include Ancient Greek {{m|grc|ἵππος}}, Sanskrit {{m|sa|अश्व|tr=áśva|sc=Deva}}, Persian [[اسب]]  ''(æsb),'' Old Armenian {{m|xcl|էշ||donkey}}, Tocharian B {{m|txb|yakwe}}, Gaulish {{m|cel-gau|epos}}.

===Pronunciation===
[[Image:Holsteiner Apfelschimmel-2005.jpg|thumb|equus (a horse)]]
* {{la-IPA|equus}}
* {{audio|la-cls-equus.ogg|Audio (Classical)|lang=la}}

===Noun===
{{la-noun|equus|equī|m|second}}

# a [[horse]]
#* {{Q|la|Virgil|Aeneid|II|48|quote='''equo''' ne credite, Teucri! Quidquid id est, timeo Danaos et dona ferentes.|trans=Do not trust '''the horse''', Trojans! Whatever it is, I fear the Danaans even if they are bearing gifts.}}
# a [[steed]], [[charger]]

